      {
        "id": 57245,
        "name": "arenson9",
        "username": "arenson9",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/arenson9/{size}/1900_2.png",
        "created_at": "2013-04-01T18:46:02.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Ronway\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/ronway/48/1856_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Ronway:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>It would be after the reduction is added. So in a sense Fixer now would negate 1 damages while being able to redirect 2 or 3 damages. Since he would also be redirecting that damage it would no longer be reduced so it'll go back to it's original value to the new target.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Technically, I believe the cannon answer is that it depends on the order the cards are played.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Although I often will short-cut the analysis process by considering possible redirections after everything else, there's nothing in the rules that says to do so.</p>",
        "post_number": 8,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T02:32:31.317Z",
        "reply_count": 0,
        "reply_to_post_number": null,
